[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## What was built[0m[0m
[0m[0m
30,500 lines of Rust, one module per C file ([38;5;10mpng.c[0m → [38;5;10mpng.rs[0m, etc.), plus [38;5;10mpngpriv.rs[0m which is the Rust form of the headers: all [38;5;10mpng_*[0m typedefs, [38;5;10mpng_struct[0m/[38;5;10mpng_info[0m, every constant, the pngpriv macros as inline functions, and the libc/zlib bindings.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The 384 exported symbols match the reference [38;5;10m.so[0m exactly — no missing, no extra. That includes the internal functions libpng also exports ([38;5;10mpng_read_data[0m, [38;5;10mpng_write_find_filter[0m, the [38;5;10mpng_sRGB_*[0m tables, …), not just the 218 documented API entries.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Key decisions[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mzlib is linked, not reimplemented.[22m libpng defers DEFLATE/INFLATE and CRC-32 to zlib, and the reference build links the system copy. Byte-identical compressed output requires the same deflate implementation, so [38;5;10mbuild.rs[0m links [38;5;10m-lz[0m too. A pure-Rust deflate (miniz_oxide) would have produced different IDAT bytes.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mextern "C-unwind"[0m instead of [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m.[22m Same C ABI; the [38;5;10m-unwind[0m variant is needed because the simplified API's [38;5;10mpng_safe_error[0m → [38;5;10mpng_safe_execute[0m pair does a second, [3minternal[23m [38;5;10msetjmp[0m/[38;5;10mlongjmp[0m that Rust cannot express soundly. That path is a Rust unwind caught by [38;5;10mcatch_unwind[0m, with a panic hook that suppresses output for our marker payload so stderr stays identical. The application-facing mechanism is unchanged: apps still [38;5;10msetjmp(png_jmpbuf(png_ptr))[0m and libpng calls their [38;5;10mlongjmp[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Verification[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Four hand-written C harnesses plus a randomised one, each compiled once and run against both [38;5;10m.so[0m files:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
| | lines compared | result |[0m[0m
|---|---|---|[0m[0m
| write/read round trips, all colour types & depths, interlacing, filters, progressive reader, simplified API, malformed inputs | 1,391 | identical |[0m[0m
| chunk get/set round trips, crc actions, unknown-chunk policies, shift/quantize/background/alpha-mode/rgb-to-gray matrices, [38;5;10mpng_read_png[0m/[38;5;10mpng_write_png[0m, truncation & bit-flip corruption, limits, flushing | 6,846 | identical |[0m[0m
| simplified API × 22 formats incl. all colour-mapped ones, file and stdio I/O, geometry edge cases (1×1 … 17×17), filter matrix, compression parameter matrix | 12,992 | identical |[0m[0m
| default (NULL) error/warning handlers, so [38;5;10mpng_default_error[0m's stderr text is compared | 20 | identical |[0m[0m
| randomised: 260 seeds × 20 iterations, random images/chunks/transforms plus mutation and truncation | ~190,000 | identical |[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Most of that was run against a Rust build with [38;5;10moverflow-checks=on[0m, which turns any wrong C integer promotion into an immediate panic rather than a silent divergence. That caught two real bugs: [38;5;10msp[1] >> gamma_shift[0m was shifting a [38;5;10mu8[0m where C promotes to [38;5;10mint[0m (overflowed whenever sBIT made [38;5;10mgamma_shift >= 8[0m), and a [38;5;10msize_t[0m subtraction in [38;5;10mpng_push_read_sig[0m that C intends to wrap.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three fuzz seeds initially disagreed. All three turned out to be libpng reading out of bounds: with a 16-bit background and [38;5;10mneed_expand=0[0m on an 8-bit or paletted image it indexes its 256-entry [38;5;10mgamma_table[0m with values like 46069. The C library's own output for those inputs changes when you shift the heap ([38;5;10mMALLOC_PERTURB_[0m, or padding the environment), so there is no defined result to match; the translation reproduces the same out-of-bounds read. Similarly, [38;5;10mpng_read_png[0m leaves the padding bits of sub-byte-depth rows uninitialised, so the harness masks them before hashing.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One difference I could not make observable but should flag: [38;5;10mpng_info_init_3[0m branches on whether the caller's claimed [38;5;10msizeof(png_info)[0m equals the real one, and the Rust [38;5;10mpng_info[0m is not the same size as the C struct. Since [38;5;10mpng_info[0m is opaque, no caller can pass the true value, so both builds take the mismatch branch — but an application that somehow knew the C size would diverge here.[0m[0m
